CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ unnamed parameters in the definition: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ swapped parameter names: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ renamed parameter in a compound type: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [temp.param]/17: `template <class C, class = C> struct S;' followed
by the definition `template <class, class> struct S' gives S<char> the
second argument char.  salvage_default_arguments copied the default
argument `C' into the definition, where no parameter has that name:
"symbol 'C' is unknown", and main was not type-checked (bin53).  The
copied default argument now names the definition's parameters, also when
they are swapped (P) or renamed (Q).  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this
program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
